WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.200
Welcome to the Whistleblower of the Week podcast hosted by FBI whistleblower Jane Turner.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:23.600
Join her as she shares the stories of real life heroes and people of integrity.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.320
Welcome to Whistleblower of the Week by the Whistleblower Network News.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:40.000
I am Jane Turner and I'm so excited to have today John Chris Kiriakou and he is somebody

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.080
that you really need to meet.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.080
He's fabulous.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.400
He was born in 1964.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.360
He's lived several lives in the last 60 years.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:56.800
He's Greek-American and former CIA and he was an intelligence analyst and operations

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.420
officer in the Central Intelligence Agency.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:07.320
He is a journalist, an author and a damn fine human being.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.320
Welcome John.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.200
I am so excited to have you on as a brother whistleblower after being off the air for several

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.200
months with fighting cancer.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.280
So I'm just delighted.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.720
I'm just delighted.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.720
Welcome.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.520
I'm very happy to see you and I'm happy to see you looking so great.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.520
Yes.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:38.800
And I will share with you after all that chemo and double mastectomy and 60 rounds of being

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.320
flashed with radiation.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.680
I have come to the decision after going through all that that whistleblowing was harder.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.640
I'm dead serious.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.640
Yeah.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.840
And so I'm going to write about that because I was left stunned as I look back, you know,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.680
the whistleblowing portion of my life was harder.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.600
And we're going to talk today about yours because you have an incredible career.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.600
Incredible.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.480
Had a good career.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.640
So look, yes, yes, and even better now, I mean, you have blown up.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.620
Let me ask you though, I am I really like to know a little bit about your background

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:27.220
and your parents because I often find that whistleblowers all share kind of the same common

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.160
background ideals and their parents are usually the same.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.700
So tell us a little bit about your mom and dad and where you came out of and.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.700
Sure.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.340
I think I should probably start with my my paternal grandparents.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.780
All four of my grandparents came from Greece from the island of Rhodes.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:54.500
My father's parents came in 1931 and my mother's parents in 1934.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:03.420
My father's parents were major players in my life.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:11.700
We were very, very close and until the day he died, my grandfather had a framed picture

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.740
of Franklin Roosevelt on the on the TV to the point where when I was a child, I thought

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.620
he was a relative of ours.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:25.740
And when I got old enough to understand, you know, what was what and who was who, my grandfather

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:31.620
said that we owed everything we had to Franklin Roosevelt because without him we could have

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.500
never remained in this country.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:39.740
We could have never become productive citizens and my grandfather had a third grade education,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.620
but both of his children ended up with PhDs.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.660
My father got a bachelor's degree in music, a master's degree in education and a PhD in

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.500
music education.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:58.660
My my aunt, Chris Anthony, got a bachelor's degree in nursing, a master's degree in nursing

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.580
and a PhD in hospital administration.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.580
So this was really the land of opportunity.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:09.620
And because of that background, when I became an adult or even when I just went away

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:18.300
to college, I only considered careers in public service because the idea was that this country

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.460
was so good to us.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.340
We had to do something to give back.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:30.840
And so I only considered the Foreign Service, the CIA, maybe Capitol Hill.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.780
I grew up in Western Pennsylvania very near my grandparents.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.500
They were constantly in our lives.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.260
They would visit every Thursday.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:44.060
We would visit them every Sunday plus every holiday we're together and we were on the phone

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.060
all the time.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.340
And I remember my grandfather, I was in fifth grade and he said, what are you reading?

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.260
And I said, it's a biography of Thomas Jefferson.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.620
He goes, why?

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.020
Biography of Alexander the Great, that's what you need to be reading.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.820
And I did as I was told and I went to the library and got the biography of Alexander

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:04.820
the Great.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.300
They were very, very impactful in my life.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:13.000
My parents were both elementary school teachers, public school teachers.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.740
My father ended up becoming a principal and was a principal for 42 years.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:22.500
My mother was a third grade and then fifth grade teacher for 22 years.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.460
And again, it was all about public service.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.860
And so when I went to school, I grew up in Amish country in Western Pennsylvania, very,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.780
very rural, very boring, nothing happening, the rust belt.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.180
Most kids sort of escaped to Pittsburgh.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.100
And I just, that wasn't far away enough for me.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:52.840
And so I developed this fascination to the point of obsession with the Middle East.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.740
When I was nine years old, I told my parents that I wanted to be a spy when I grew

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:58.740
up.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.180
And they thought that was cute.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.820
And then at Christmas, they gave me walkie-talkies so my brother and I would, you know, walk around

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.060
the neighborhood talking on our walkie-talkies.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.860
But when I was 16, I told my father specifically that I wanted to be a spy in the Middle

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:13.860
East.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:19.860
And he said, still with the spy business, can't you be like a dentist or something?

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.940
And I said, dad, I want to live in the Middle East and I really want to be a spy.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.660
And I became a spy in the Middle East.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.500
I went to George Washington University.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.620
I got a degree in Middle Eastern studies.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:35.020
I stayed for a master's degree in legislative affairs, but with a focus in American foreign

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.620
policy analysis.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.860
And then my grad school advisor recruited me into the CIA.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:43.900
I spent the first seven and a half years of my career as an analyst on Iraq.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.780
I did overseas tours in Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and Bahrain.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.420
And then I switched to counterterrorist operations.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.940
And I did an operational tour in Athens.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.280
Then I became the chief of counterterrorist operations in Pakistan.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:03.760
And then I finished my headquarters career as the executive assistant to the CIA's deputy

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.840
director for operations.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.200
And then my final tour was at the United Nations.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.400
I was assigned to the UN Security Council.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.360
And then called it a career.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.920
And I have a question about that, John, because I see that you were given just the best

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.600
ratings that you can get in the CIA.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.000
You were given awards.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:28.720
I got 12 exceptional performance awards.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.240
Right.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:34.800
The Sustained Superior Performance Award and the Meritorious Honor Award with medal.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.040
You were, yeah, you were no slump.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.040
I mean, and here you were in the middle.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.360
Yes, you did.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.480
And they recognized you for that.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.480
So they thought extremely highly of you.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.240
And here you are, chief of counterterrorist operations in Pakistan.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:54.920
I mean, some of the most volatile, you know, important postings.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.960
Why did you walk away from that?

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.800
Especially since from just a little kid, that's what you wanted to be a spy.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.200
The the the real reason that I walked away, I resigned after 15 years

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.040
because I had gotten divorced and my sons were young.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.200
They were seven and four.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.360
And, you know, kids need their dad, especially little boys.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.440
And my ex-wife went back home to Ohio.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.040
And I just couldn't bear the thought of living without my kids.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:31.280
Yeah.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.040
So I decided to resign and go into the public sector, the private sector.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:39.240
Rather, I went to work for the big four accounting firm of Deloitte and Tush.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:44.360
I was the deputy deputy director of their competitive intelligence practice.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.680
And so they told me in the interview process,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.640
we don't care where you sit when you're writing the report.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:55.040
I mean, if you want to be at your folks' house in Pennsylvania

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:58.880
or at a Starbucks, you know, around the corner from your kids' house,

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.520
that's fine with us.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.080
And so I resigned from the agency and went to work for Deloitte.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.440
It was the most boring job I ever had in my life.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.160
But it was the best group of people that I ever worked with in my life.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.200
And I did that for almost four years.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:21.560
Until fate intervened and everything changed.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.800
Yes. And fate did intervene.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.440
You know, I think it's interesting that the person who solicited you for the CIA

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.880
was a psychiatrist. Yeah.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.640
So he must have passed judgment that you were very well adjusted.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.880
You know, as exact words were,

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.160
he was looking for people who would fit into the CIA's culture.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:46.720
And he said, you would fit into the CIA's culture.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.880
Exactly. And you did.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.160
You were just a superstar. I thrived.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.280
You were a superstar.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.920
So so the family, which, of course, you value that more than anything, which.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.160
makes you a very honorable man as you know. Thank you. You had five sons the same as Thomas Drake,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:13.040
which I think is four sons and a daughter. Yeah, and he has five sons. Yeah. And so you share some

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:20.000
commonality there. We do. Which I think is great. Tom Drake. Tom Drake is one of the finest human

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.360
beings I've ever had the pleasure of encountering in my life. And I met him through the great

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:36.000
Jaisalyn Radak just days after my arrest in 2012. And Tom was one of those handful of people

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:44.240
who I credit for saving my life. I could not have gotten through that awful experience

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:54.000
without the wisdom and advice and friendship of Tom Drake. Yeah, he's amazing. And now you enter

00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:01.520
his same rank. I mean, I just am so proud of you too. And it's just stunning as far as I can see.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:07.040
But then here again, many lives you've led and you've been put in these situations

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:13.120
which lead me to ask you, do you believe in divine intervention? I mean,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.720
you have been in the right place at the right time, even though at the time it must

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:24.960
looked terrible. But looking back, do you believe you were in the... That's the key right there,

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:34.960
looking back. Yeah. Because at the time, I was raised in a pious church going Greek Orthodox

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:43.360
household. And I remember saying to God, am I really that bad a guy that I deserve this?

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:50.720
Yeah. The whole U.S. government falls on my head. Yeah. Three counts of espionage

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:57.840
for having lunch with the New York Times and speaking to ABC News about a program that was

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:03.520
clearly illegal. Yes. But you know, it's funny, Jane, because

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.840
if you... I give people this advice all the time, things really do get better.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:18.640
They really do. And if you know in your heart, in your gut that you're right and the other side

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:24.240
is wrong, you're going to be okay. Exactly. And that's what's happened. Before we started

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:30.160
recording, I mentioned to you that my detractors at the CIA who were very powerful and very

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:38.960
dangerous men. Yes. And one woman are all now either dead or retired. They're gone.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:50.400
And I'm the one who still commands time in front of the TV cameras or in front of the podcast

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:57.520
microphones. It's my side of the story. That's the side of record. And in your voice.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:05.280
And in your voice. No one's interpreting your case for you. It's your voice which is arriving

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.560
at the other side, which is just thrilling in your case. I just thrilling. But now you do share.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:18.880
You did go to jail. And long time. And I'm sure

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:27.360
knowing the type of person you are, it was no technique. No. In fact, actually just

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:33.760
coincidentally, I wrote a book about it while I was in doing time like a spy. How the CIA

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:40.640
taught me to survive and thrive in prison, which interestingly enough, won two literary awards.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:46.800
I wrote it in long hand on legal paper. And every once in a while, the guards would come into my

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:54.720
cell and confiscate it and destroy it. So they said it was damaging to prison morale.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:03.200
And so what I ended up doing was writing every day. And every day when I was done writing,

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:09.040
I would put it in an envelope, market legal mail, eyes only, which meant that they couldn't

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:15.520
intercept it. And I would send it to Jaisal and Radak. And she saved dozens upon dozens of

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:23.840
these envelopes. And when I got out of prison, I took them all. I ended up repeating a lot of

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:28.320
the book because I couldn't remember what I had already written or was it confiscated or was

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:36.640
it not confiscated? So I paid a Georgetown University undergrad $500 just to type it all onto a thumb drive

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:48.000
and then I edited it and I sent it to the publisher. But yeah, on my very first day,

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:55.840
it was clear to me, you know what, before my first day, one of my attorneys took me aside

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:00.880
and he said, listen, I'm going to say something important and I need for you to understand it.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:09.680
He said, the CIA is very upset that you only got 23 months. Yes. They're going to try to set you up

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:14.400
and trap you into doing something because they want you in there for at least five years.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:21.600
So beware. And I said, okay. And I was there about

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:32.960
four months and a guy in my housing unit, an Afghan American pharmacist with an unfortunate

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:40.720
Oxycontin problem came up to me. Nice, nice guy. And he said to me, hey, John, there's a guy

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:46.720
that wants to meet you. He's the spokesman for the Taliban. And I said, the spokesman for the

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:52.320
Taliban, are you talking about that case from New Jersey? And he said, yeah, yeah, that guy.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:58.640
I said, I don't have any desire to talk to that guy. And he said, oh, okay, I'll tell him a few

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:04.400
days later, I'm in the yard walking around the track. And this guy with a beard halfway down

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:10.720
to his waist is walking toward me with his hand stretched out, outstretched to shake my hands.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:17.120
And I put on my hands and I said, don't touch me. And I looked just past him.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:23.840
And there was a guard in the woods outside the fence with a camera with a long distance

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:27.600
telephoto lens. Click, click, click, click, click, click, click, click. So my hands were up

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:33.040
and I said, don't you dare touch me. I will drop you where you stand. And he said,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:40.000
come on, man, we have a lot in common. And I said, we have nothing in common other than the

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:44.880
fact that I spent my career trying to kill people like you. I said, that's right off.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:50.880
And he put his hand down and finally walked away. So I was like this the whole time I never

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:56.320
touched him, never shook his hand. Then the cop ran away from his little hiding post.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:01.120
And just two days later, that prisoner was transferred out. He was in our prison

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:05.920
for six days, six days. He was there to set you up, to set me up.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:12.000
But let's, let's, I get too far away from the reason you ended up in jail,

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:17.520
along with other prestigious whistleblowers like a reality winner. And of course,

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:23.840
Thomas Drake didn't actually go to jail, but he suffered tremendously. Natalie Edwards,

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:31.360
who was the Finnson whistleblower, all ended up in jail because they took the Righteous Road.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:38.480
Let's talk about the Righteous Road you took, which ended you up in jail. And it's something

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:45.200
so horrific. I just would think the people on the government side would hang their heads in

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.920
shame. Tell us about what you blew.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:57.040
Congress passed a landmark law to protect whistleblowers who expose money laundering,

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:03.120
cartel financing, and terrorist networks. Now Finnson is proposing rules that gut it,

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.880
illegal barriers, no protection for international sources,

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:13.440
and restrictions Congress never authorized. If Finnson ignores the legal objections raised,

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:18.560
the rules can be challenged in court. But the public comment period is still open.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:23.520
The window closes June 1st. If you believe these whistleblowers deserve real protection,

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.800
your comment will become part of the permanent legal record.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:29.680
Submit your comment today at whistleblowers.org.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:37.600
Whistle on. I blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:43.920
I had been the Chief of Counterterrorism Operations in Pakistan after 9-11, and I was

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:50.000
responsible for the capture of the very first high-value target, Zayn al-Abdin Muhammad Hussain,

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.360
who was known as Abu Zubaydah. We believed at the time that Abu Zubaydah was the number three

00:18:55.360 --> 00:19:00.800
in al-Qaeda. He wasn't. He was a bad guy, but he was not the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:04.480
He had nothing to do with 9-11. Well,

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.680
I had no idea what was going to happen to Abu Zubaydah.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:15.040
Once we caught him, I turned him over to a rendition team and they disappeared into the

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:21.280
sky on the CIA's private jet. And then a couple of months later, I returned to headquarters.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:27.680
It was at headquarters in the cafeteria, no less, that a senior officer approached me

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:32.880
and asked me very casually if I wanted to be trained in the use of enhanced interrogation

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:38.640
techniques. I'd never heard the term before. No. He told me what they were. I said,

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:44.080
that is a torture program. And very defensively, he said, it's not a torture program.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:48.480
DOJ approved it, and the president signed it, and we're going to get rough with these guys.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:55.200
So I told them that I had a moral and ethical problem with it, and I believed that it was

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:59.840
illegal. I didn't care what the justice department's office of-

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.800
of legal counsel said, OLC, this was an illegal program.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:13.480
I was the only person out of 14 who were approached who declined the torture training

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:21.480
and then the VEDA was tortured mercilessly beginning on August the 2nd, 2002.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:28.600
So I kept waiting for somebody to go public and to say that this was happening and indeed

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:35.240
someone blew the whistle on the secret prisons that the CIA had set up.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:40.480
There was a woman, a woman whom I respected deeply who was at the White House.

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:44.640
They escorted her out of the building, took her badge, told her never to come back.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:48.680
But to this day, she adamantly says that it was not her.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.040
Whoever it was, they're an American hero.

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:52.040
Right.

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.040
We'll never know who it was.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.040
Right.

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:57.320
Okay.

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:03.880
In December of 2007, Brian Ross called me from ABC News.

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:06.480
Nobody had gone public over Abu Zubeda.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:10.320
But Brian said that he had a source who said that I had tortured Abu Zubeda.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:12.840
I said that was absolutely untrue.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.520
I was the only person who was kind to Abu Zubeda.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.640
And he said, well, you're welcome to come on the show and defend yourself.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.080
I had never spoken to a journalist before.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.360
I didn't know that was an old reporter's trick.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.680
I said, I'll think about it.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:32.440
And then in the intervening days, that was on a Monday.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:37.360
On Wednesday, President George W. Bush gave a press conference in which he said, he

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:42.920
looked in the camera and he said, we do not torture.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:46.680
And I said to my wife, my second wife, she was a senior CIA officer.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.960
I said, he is a bald faced liar.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:55.440
He's just looking at the American people in the eye and he's just lying to them.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:01.440
And then on Friday, the president comes out of the South Portico of the White House walking

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:06.520
toward the helicopter, Marine One, to fly to Camp David for the weekend.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:10.960
And a reporter shouted a question at him about torture and he stopped.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:16.680
And he said, well, if there is torture, it's because of a rogue CIA officer.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.640
And I said to my wife, Brian Ross's source is at the White House.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:22.600
And they're going to try to pin this on me.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:24.760
I'm going public.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:30.320
And so I called Brian and I said, I will give you your interview.

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:41.040
And I decided in the whatever it was four or five days between Bush's statement and

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:44.200
the interview that I would simply tell the truth.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:53.400
And so I went to ABC News here in Washington and I said three things.

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:57.080
I mean, I said a lot of things, but I said three things that were important.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:01.360
I said that torture was official US government policy.

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:05.000
It was not the result of a rogue.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:08.640
I said the CIA was indeed torturing its prisoners.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.560
And I said that the torture program had been personally approved by the president

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:15.000
himself.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.440
And then the whole government just fell on my head.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:19.680
Yes.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.080
Yes.

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:26.480
Because you feel or you revealed rocks in the soul of America, didn't you?

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:32.400
Yeah, I mean, that was a shocker that our country could do something like that.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:35.640
You know, just where the shining city on the exactly.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:36.800
And I say that all the time.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:40.680
Ronald Reagan said we were a shining city on a hill.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:47.880
We were a beacon of hope for the rest of the world because we had respect for human rights

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.560
and civil rights and civil liberties.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:51.560
Right.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:52.960
But we didn't.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:58.400
You know, when I was overseas, I was on rotation to the State Department for three

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:02.560
years and twice I was the human rights officer.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:07.040
So I wrote the annual human rights report that went to Congress and I took that job

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:08.040
seriously.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:09.040
Yes.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:15.680
I remember going into the Minister of Interior's office in Bahrain and saying, Your Highness,

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:23.000
you cannot murder a 15 year old child because he marched in a pro-democracy demonstration.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:24.560
A peaceful one at that.

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:29.680
I have to report this to Congress and they could cut off arms sales.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.720
You have to respect human rights.

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:38.400
Well then, five years later, 9-11 happens, six years later, and then what?

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:42.200
Then CIA station chiefs all around the world are going in saying, Don't pay attention to

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:43.920
the human rights guy.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:48.320
We want you to open a secret prison and we're going to torture people there.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:52.520
Or better yet, you torture them and then just give us transcripts of whatever they

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:53.520
say.

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:54.520
Yeah.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:56.680
What kind of shining city on a hill is that?

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:57.680
It isn't.

00:24:57.680 --> 00:24:59.560
It isn't.

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:05.200
And you know what makes me really sad, really sad, John, is the fact that that

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:11.160
gives now every other government in the world the right to do it to our people.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:12.160
Exactly.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:17.160
It's not an accident, Jane, that even ISIS, when they would kidnap an American, and

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:23.480
they did kidnap American journalists, they would make them wear orange jumpsuits or beheading

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:24.480
them.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:25.480
Yeah.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:28.440
And it's because we made our prisoners wear orange jumpsuits.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:29.840
Right.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:33.760
And then those horrific pictures that came out, you know, where they had them naked

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:40.360
and yes, scaring them with dogs and it just does not meet the standards that this nation

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:42.800
has so long exposed.

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:44.560
But then here you are.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:49.760
You're riding the whirlwind because they must have revealed your name because they came

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:50.760
after you.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:51.760
Or did they not?

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:52.760
Right.

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:53.760
They did reveal it.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.760
I'm 100 percent sure that they had revealed my name.

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:56.760
Yes.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:57.760
Okay.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:58.760
Boom.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:25:59.760
There you are.

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:00.760
Yeah.

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:01.760
Did you have legal representation during that time?

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:02.760
I'm glad you asked that.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:03.760
Yeah.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.760
Did you rush into it and just didn't have any legal?

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:12.840
This is the most important advice that I can possibly give any would-be whistleblower.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:14.960
Don't make the mistake I made.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.560
I just went and did the interview.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:24.400
The only thing I would change in this entire experience is don't open your mouth unless

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:29.600
an attorney is sitting next to you and preferably an attorney skilled in whistleblower

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:31.840
defense.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:32.840
Amen.

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:33.840
Don't say a word.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:34.840
A word.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:35.840
Amen, brother.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:36.840
And you learned the hard way, didn't you?

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:37.840
Oh, boy, did I.

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:38.840
Oh, boy.

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:39.840
I had to be reactive, Jane.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:40.840
Yeah.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:42.760
Now, granted, I ended up with the best lawyer's money could buy.

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:46.040
The Washington Post called my attorneys legal titans.

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:47.040
Yeah.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:49.400
I mean A-list attorneys.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:50.560
Right.

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:52.720
But still, I was reactive.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:54.080
Exactly.

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.120
And that's what happened to Reality Winner and Natalie Edwards.

00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.120
That's right.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.480
It makes you so sad because they have the best intentions.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:12.520
They have a moral compass, which is pointing north like you, and they're concerned about

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:19.440
treatment of other people and how our nation is not living up to what it should be.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.480
And it's not justice, John.

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:26.640
Now, correct me if I'm wrong, but the government wasn't seeking justice in your case.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:34.440
It seems to me they were looking at retaliation, reprisal, retribution, and just punishing you.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.440
Punish you.

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.440
Punish you.

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:42.000
My attorneys were unanimous in their belief, and they used to say this to me all the time.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.120
This case is not about John Kiriakou.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:46.240
You're small potatoes for them.

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:50.800
This case is about frightening any other would-be whistleblower, whether it's in

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:57.880
the CIA, or the FBI, or NSA, or even the Pentagon, frightening them into silence.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:00.040
You see what we did to Kiriakou?

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:01.280
He's bankrupt.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.360
He lost his family.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.000
He's going to go to prison.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:06.440
He lost his pension.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:11.800
You want to be like him, or you want to just keep your mouth shut?

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:15.480
And the majority of people keep their mouth shut, John.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:19.880
This is what makes it so interesting and why I love talking to whistleblowers is

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:26.600
because they're unique, and they possess certain characteristics that do not allow them to keep

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.600
silent.

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:36.600
They have a driving need to reveal injustice, and torture is injustice.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:37.600
It's injustice.

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:46.120
Jane, forgive me for preaching or pontificating, but we have a law in this country called

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:54.520
the Federal Torture Act of 1946, which bans by name exactly those torture techniques that

00:28:54.520 --> 00:29:00.680
we used against prisoners, besides the fact that we executed Japanese soldiers who had

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:03.360
waterboarded American POWs.

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:08.680
We're the authors of the United Nations Convention Against Torture.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:12.240
We can't just pretend that these laws don't exist.

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:13.480
Right.

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:15.480
And we have fallen down, haven't we?

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:16.560
Yes.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:24.000
Do you see us still down on the ground in terms of the morality, the justice aspect?

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.320
Do you think we're still down on that ground?

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:27.320
I do.

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:28.920
I do believe that we're still down.

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:31.960
And I think there's a lot of blame to go around.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:37.600
Certainly whoever happens to be occupying the White House on any given day or in

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:40.360
any given year, and this is not a partisan issue.

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:43.720
It doesn't matter if it's a Republican or a Democrat in the White House.

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:48.760
They're weak when it comes to civil rights, civil liberties and human rights.

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:57.000
But I put equal blame on Capitol Hill because we have oversight committees, oversight committees,

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:00.000
that really, as you well know, Jane, they're...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.840
they're no more than cheerleaders for the likes of the CIA, the FBI, really the entire intelligence

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:15.600
community, the defense community, they don't oversee anything. No. They just rubber stamp

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:21.920
everything. Rubber stamp, that's what they do. Yeah, and we've seen that, haven't we?

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:25.920
The other thing I'd like to point out to our audience, which is extremely important,

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:34.400
is that you have helped other whistleblowers. I know one personally that he was not in good

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:38.880
shape. As you know, some whistleblowers are able to handle things a little bit better than others,

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:47.280
and the majority of us just are beat to death. We really are. But I know you put out your hand

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:53.920
and you kept him from slipping down. Boy, that's a wonderful thing to do.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:31:00.960
It was the least I could do, and it's not worthy of thanks. I had so many people offer me a helping

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:07.280
hand when I was at my lowest. I knew that he was struggling mentally, emotionally. The pressure

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:17.200
is all consuming, and it was just swallowing him up. If you can help somebody just by

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.360
getting him out of the house for three or four hours or go into dinner,

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:28.000
it's the least you can do. Like I say, so many people helped me. So many people reached out

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:35.040
proactively to help me. It was the least I could do. I got an email from an attorney today this

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:40.160
morning asking me if I can help another whistleblower. I've got the email right here in front

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:45.120
of me asking if I can help another whistleblower, and I said, absolutely yes, I absolutely

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:50.480
brought up your name, and Jesselyn Radek's name, and Tom Drake and a handful of other people.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:53.600
That's right. And I'm going to put everybody in touch. I'm going to do that today.

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:58.320
Yeah, and we're more than happy to do that, as you know, because that's part of our mission

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:02.800
now, isn't it? That's part of our journey is helping others. That's right. Because all of

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:08.960
us can remember how alone we felt and how desperate things seemed because the whole

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.800
government is just kicking the dust bunnies out of you.

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:19.360
Yes, indeed. Absolutely true. Oh, yeah. And they've blackballed you in your industry?

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:23.520
Oh, yeah. They're just vicious. Yeah, yeah, yeah. Vicious. Oh, it's never ending.

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:28.240
I commend you. I commend you. Thank you. I'll tell you why I thought so hard.

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:34.960
Daniel Ellsberg, who of course is the godfather of all national security whistleblowers

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:42.800
and a genuine and bona fide hero to all of us, Dan said something to me that was life changing.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:48.320
Dan blew the whistle on the Vietnam War with release of the Pentagon Papers in 1970.

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:54.480
The Nixon administration not only ruined his life, but they tried to get him to commit suicide.

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:59.680
They charged him with espionage, but he was never tried because they went so far as to

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:07.760
break into his psychiatrist's office, steal his psychiatric records, and then just release them

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:16.720
to the media. And the judge threw the charges out. Dan told me that for all intents and purposes,

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:25.680
he never worked again. He said he had been poor since 1970. And it was only because of the

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:33.280
generosity and kindness of his brother-in-law, his wife's brother. His wife's parents cut them off

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:40.560
cold turkey because they were Nixon followers. And Dan brought shame on the family and all the same

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:48.560
crap we've all heard. And so it was only because of the generosity of his wife's brother that they

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:55.200
were able to survive. And he said to me, his exact words were no national security whistleblower

00:33:55.200 --> 00:34:00.800
has ever made a financial comeback. By God, Dan, that is not going to be me.

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:06.880
I'm going to fight until I do make a comeback. I'm not going to let them have the last laugh.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:14.880
And so I've been relentless. Yes, you have. Relentless. And I just love what you're doing.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:22.640
Not only are you relentless, and you're trying to restore integrity to our government,

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:27.280
but you are showing a path for other whistleblowers because you're right.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:32.960
I hope so. The government wants to beat you up so badly that other people who are thinking of maybe

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:40.800
telling the truth are like, no, can't do that. I mean, you lose families. You lose your job.

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:47.920
I even had a cousin, a cousin older than I, whom I had always respected.

00:34:48.640 --> 00:34:55.760
And he told me that he just couldn't speak to me anymore. First cousin, because I brought shame

00:34:55.760 --> 00:35:01.280
on our family. That's what he said. And I said, I brought shame on our family. I said, you imported

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:07.280
drugs from the Colombians, and I brought shame on our family. I said, you think I didn't see

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:12.800
your name in the Washington Post as the Coke dealer to the mayor of Alexandria, Virginia,

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:17.120
and that you ran to Greece to hide until the statute of limitations was expired?

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:21.520
You think I didn't know about that? And I'm the one who brought shame to our family.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:27.440
Yeah. Every whistleblower has that story. I had a relative who said, well, why would the FBI do

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:33.520
that if it wasn't true? Well, as people are finding out, yeah, they do do these things.

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:41.360
Oh boy. Yes, they do. Yes, they do. So tell me your future because you are a rock star right now.

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:45.760
Thank you. You're a rock star. I've been very, very fortunate.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:50.240
You're coming back. So this is what your dream was. You're going to come back,

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:53.680
and you have been. Yeah. So what is your future going to be now?

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:59.280
Well, it took me 12 years to become an overnight success. That's what my brother says.

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:08.880
When I got out of prison, I literally could not find a job anywhere. I mean,

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:19.360
I was turned down by Target and Safeway and McDonald's and Uber. We don't hire felons.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:28.960
We don't hire felons. I literally could not find a job anywhere. And so I finally got a crumb

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:33.600
from a think tank in Washington called the Institute for Policy Studies.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:39.680
So they offered me a job that paid minimum wage, but I would have to have an ongoing

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:45.120
GoFundMe to raise the money for my own salary. I did that for a year, and then they said,

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:53.680
this is untenable, best of luck. And so I left. I had twice turned down an offer of a radio show

00:36:53.680 --> 00:37:01.680
at Sputnik Radio, which is a radio network owned by the Russian government. But by then,

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:09.440
my wife and I had split up. And it was either do that or go on food stamps.

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:20.320
Quite literally. So I said to the Russians, if I took this job, I would want the freedom

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:24.480
to criticize anybody I wanted, including Vladimir Putin. They said, done.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:29.600
I said, put it in writing in the contract. And they said, done. And they did it. So I

00:37:29.600 --> 00:37:34.560
signed it. I ended up spending seven years there. And over the course of seven years,

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:41.520
I started doing my own podcast and I started teaching as an adjunct at a handful of universities.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:51.520
And things started to gain a little traction. And then Joe Biden put sanctions on the Russian

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:59.600
media and I lost my job. Yep. And so I had a couple of bucks saved, like not much,

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:06.720
couple of bucks. And I decided I'm going to start my own podcast with a friend. Ended up

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:14.560
doing three podcasts. I've since left one of them. But I was able to monetize all three of them.

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:23.520
And I would supplement that with speeches, paid speeches all around the world. I'm far more popular

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:28.000
in Europe and the Middle East than I am in the United States. So I was making a good

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:36.960
bit of money overseas. And then two months ago, I gave an interview to a very popular

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:46.480
podcaster, Stephen Barlett, who does the diary of a CEO. And a college student in Texas took that

00:38:46.480 --> 00:38:51.120
interview and chopped it up into shorts and gave me a funny Alvin and the chipmunks voice and had

00:38:51.120 --> 00:39:00.400
lasers shooting out of my eyes. And it just scored like an atomic bomb with Gen Z. And the next

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:09.200
thing I know, I'm on TV everywhere and I'm doing cameo recordings. I can't keep up with cameos.

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:16.080
This morning, let me look, what am I up to now that are still pending? 111 are still pending.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:25.120
Wow. It's I can't do it all. But that led to me being signed by the biggest talent agency in the

00:39:25.120 --> 00:39:30.880
world, which includes a speakers bureau and it includes an A list literary agent. I've written

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:36.800
nine books that was that also has supplemented my income. Well, instead of my book selling,

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:45.280
you know, 1000 copies, 3000 copies, my first one sold 150,000 copies and we're going back to

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:51.680
the big five again. So there is life after whistleblowing. Dan Ellsberg was wrong.

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:59.920
Exactly. Yeah. Exactly. And that's so important to get that word out, isn't it? You don't have to go out and hide in the

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.680
woods anymore. You don't have to do that. And, you know, as long as we are able to facilitate

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:12.120
each other, to help each other, you know, I think we're going to see more and more stories

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:14.480
like yours. I think you're right.

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:19.600
Yes. And now I understand you're going to National Whistleblower Day possibly in July.

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:24.920
I have to tell you, I love National Whistleblower Day. It's an event that the National Whistleblower

00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:32.320
Center puts on every year. It's always a major event on Capitol Hill. It's always held in

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:38.320
the Kennedy Caucus Room, which is not just, you know, the biggest room on Capitol Hill.

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:46.040
It's the most historic single room anywhere on Capitol Hill. It's where every major

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:52.400
Senate hearing from the sinking of the Titanic to Watergate and Iran Contra has

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:59.440
been held. And you pack that place every single year. It's a fantastic day. It's a

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:05.360
fantastic lunch. And then you get, you know, A-list movers and shakers from

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:10.320
Capitol Hill. Senators, congressmen, committee chairman to come and celebrate

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:15.120
whistleblowers. It's an event that no whistleblower or whistleblowers

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:18.280
supporters should miss.

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:20.200
Well, that's why I'm so excited you're coming this year.

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:22.320
I am too. And Tom Drake came last year.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:29.440
Yes, indeed. And I was just so impressed with him. It's just incredible. So what would

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:35.080
you tell that little boy who told his dad years and years ago that he wanted to

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:41.080
be a spy? If you had the chance to travel back, what would you tell him?

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:45.960
Always tell the truth and do the right thing, which is exactly what I was

00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:52.240
raised to do. You know, I say all the time, Jane, we all know in our gut the

00:41:52.240 --> 00:41:57.680
difference between right and wrong. It's just a basic human understanding. The

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:03.200
question is, do we then do what's right? Or do we do what's easy? Do we do

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:09.960
what's expedient? So I would tell that little boy, your parents and

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:16.760
grandparents were right. Do what's right and always tell the truth. And I think

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:24.080
that's why I'm resonating with this generation of people between 15 and

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:27.480
30, primarily, because I tell the truth.

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:37.880
And that's become so rare these days. So yeah, I just am delighted. And here's

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:43.200
the big one. If you had to do it over again, would you do it?

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:48.600
Oh, yes. That's an easy question. The only thing I would do differently is have

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:53.640
that lawyer sitting right next to me. That is it. But yes, I would

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:55.040
absolutely do it again.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:05.680
And John, considering you are just a person of extreme integrity and truth,

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:10.480
what are your last words that you're going to part with here to our audience?

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:14.880
Oh, thank you. I want people to know if you're thinking of blowing the

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:19.240
whistle on waste, fraud, abuse, illegality or threats to the public

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:27.040
health or public safety, you are not alone. There is an entire community of

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:32.440
supporters out there waiting for you. Don't be afraid to reach out and

00:43:32.440 --> 00:43:38.800
ask for help or ask for guidance. The support is there and it's waiting for

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:39.120
you.

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:45.120
Wonderful. And does it scare you, John, that you may be entering those same

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:53.960
ranks as Snowden, Drake? Does that frighten you at all? Or do you embrace it?

00:43:54.400 --> 00:44:03.640
It's funny, Jane. We're all friends now. I participated in an event in Berlin

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:10.440
two or three weeks ago. And it's an event that I've done two or three times in

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:17.880
the past. So I go to the hotel and I check in and the woman says, I don't

00:44:17.880 --> 00:44:21.120
see you on the computer. Are you on this list for the conference? And she

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:24.800
gives me a list of a dozen people and every single one of them is a friend

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:30.080
of mine. It was Tom Drake and Jaisalyn Radak and Terry Albury and

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:35.960
Oh, Terry. Yeah. Yeah. And Lisa Ling and just everybody on the list was a

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:41.760
whistleblower and an outspoken whistleblower at that. It was

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:43.920
fantastic. Isn't that great?

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:49.120
Loved it. And I see nothing but just wonderful things happening to

00:44:49.120 --> 00:44:53.120
you in the future, John. Oh, thank you. From your lips to God's ears.

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:56.760
Thank you. I appreciate it. Thank you for joining our podcast,

00:44:56.760 --> 00:45:00.520
Whistleblower of the Week. You are a hero. Thank you so much for

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:01.840
everything. Thank you.

